{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/296778-help-me-understand-timeline-assumptions-for-a-chronicle","id":296778,"title":"help me understand... Timeline Assumptions for a Chronicle ..?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-30T01:56:23-07:00","id":296778,"title":"help me understand... Timeline Assumptions for a Chronicle ..?","content":"Greetings all,<br /> <br /> (again) I've been fascinated by the unique style of storytelling that <i>Mummy</i>, and I'm thinking about putting together a chronicle to run sometime next year, so I've been reading up on it. I'm confused about a few things though, I and was hoping the community can help me figure it out (or point to the right sections to read). My primary questions (in this thread) are what <i>Mummy</i> assumes is the typical timeline for a chronicle, and about character progression.<br /> <br /> The character creation rules finish off the process by giving 20 exp to starting characters; like always, they recommend giving more to &quot;higher level&quot; games - 75 is for veterans of the Descent. Do these guidelines assume the main story is going to be in the 2012 Sothic Cycle? I would think that any Arisen that makes it to the 4th cycle would be considered a veteran, and have reached higher Memory... This is probably just the typical guide for any game: &quot;whenever you're running, this is the default; this is for higher-powered&quot;. But part of me is thinking that it would make sense to give, like, 20 starting experience per Sothic Cycle they've been through, or something. Maybe I'm reading too much into things. But it gets to another question.<br /> <br /> <i>Mummy</i> uses flashbacks a lot as part of its storytelling style; I'm wondering how extensive that is. For example, would it make sense (or is it the default assumption) that the overarching story of a chronicle would take place in 2012, but mostly be used as framing; most of the individual stories taking place in past descents, skipping up and down the timeline to whatever short story arc is interesting, while seeding knowledge about the overarching conflict, resolved far in the present?<br /> Or is it intended that flashbacks are brief but frequent - primarily scenes of self-discovery, but often plot relevant - however the story arcs themselves and the bulk of plot / dramatic action occurs on the same timeline?<br /> While I'm certain that both of these styles of game are possible, I'm curious what the core <i>assumes</i> as the default, and which might take extra tinkering and present some clashes with the published material.<br /> <br /> That question segues nicely into my second big question, which is about how character progression and spending experience works. It seems clear to me that flashback scenes make great opportunities to earn exp and justify buying new traits. It works well for those dramatic moments where the player reveals they have a secret advantage that turns the scene, as we're so fond of seeing in novels and such. But traits bought in the present (or the latest period timeline)... are they available in flashback scenes?<br /> A lot of social merits (allies, contacts) don't logically transfer across time periods; sanctity of merits would suggest that they always can be used, they just apply to different people each time period, but that seems like an odd allowance, given that it entails cultivating relationships, and Arisen don't always have the chance to. I guess their cults do, but it seems like a bit of stretch to have in the charter to secure certain kinds of social connections, prepared enough in advance that they won't freak out when a high-Sekhem Arisen calls in a favor during an unexpected awakening. There is the &quot;use your best judgment of when the merit's available&quot; philosophy - and that's fine, but it seems like it could also lead to a lot of re-configuring of merits.<br /> From the basic chronology of characters, it makes most sense for Arisen to have a linear experience gains / character growth. Narratively, it makes sense to put more challenging scenes later in the chronicle, no matter when in the timeline it's flashing to. Locking characters out of skills seems unfair and like a lot of bookkeeping, even if its reasonable in a chronological sense, and letting purchases in the future apply to the past seems weird.<br /> <br /> <br /> ...I'm not sure where these questions are going. I feel like I've sort of answered myself just by asking them, and like I'm trying to complicate things more than they need to be just by pondering them. I'm just feeling like there are some bumps in this storytelling style, and I'm feeling shakey on the most appropriate ways of handling them. If the assumption that the bulk of the action should be sequential in the same timeline, these probably won't be problems. If you want a chronicle with major stories in 551 and one in 2012, then run 551 first. But <i>Mummy</i> lends itself to staggering back and forth on the timeline, and that's a really cool feature I'd like to play around with; it just seems to come with bumpy considerations, at the very least.<br /> So, in the end here, I don't know if I actually have questions, so much as a concerned ramble, and the hope for some kind of discussion about different ways to handle these details - and how much it really matters.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>TLDR:</b> I think I want to play around with timelines and flashbacks that the game system doesn't expect by default (story-long flashbacks, moving randomly up and down the timeline), and will introduce some mechanical complication in terms of character progression. I'd appreciate discussion about others' game experiences and what tinkering has been tried to help me wrap my head around running a timey-wimey kind of chronicle.<br /> <br /> Thanks,<br /> ~Seraph Kitty","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":425,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","user":"Seraph Kitty","date":". 10-30-2014, 03:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-30T05:35:26-07:00","id":296991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seraph Kitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/296778-help-me-understand-timeline-assumptions-for-a-chronicle#post296778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The character creation rules finish off the process by giving 20 exp to starting characters; like always, they recommend giving more to &quot;higher level&quot; games - 75 is for veterans of the Descent. Do these guidelines assume the main story is going to be in the 2012 Sothic Cycle? I would think that any Arisen that makes it to the 4th cycle would be considered a veteran, and have reached higher Memory... I would think this is just the typical guide for any game: &quot;whenever you're running, this is the default; this is for higher-powered&quot;. But part of me is thinking that it would make sense to give, like, 20 starting experience per Sothic Cycle they've been through, or something. Maybe I'm reading too much into things. But it gets to another question.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't overthink this. A 20xp bonus is the default for the 2012 Sothic cycle, and I would just handwave each cycle previous being 5xp less. So 15xp in 600ad, 10xp in 800bc, and 5xp in 2200bc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seraph Kitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/296778-help-me-understand-timeline-assumptions-for-a-chronicle#post296778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>Mummy</i> uses flashbacks a lot as part of its storytelling style; I'm wondering how extensive that is. For example, would it make sense (or is it the default assumption) that the overarching story of a chronicle would take place in 2012, but mostly be used as framing; most of the individual stories taking place in past descents, skipping up and down the timeline to whatever short story arc is interesting, while seeding knowledge about the overarching conflict, resolved far in the present?<br /> Or is it intended that flashbacks are brief but frequent - primarily scenes of self-discovery, but often plot relevant - however the story arcs themselves and the bulk of plot / dramatic action occurs on the same timeline?<br /> While I'm certain that both of these styles of game are possible, I'm curious what the core <i>assumes</i> as the default, and which might take extra tinkering and present some clashes with the published material.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is up to you. I've read or listened to APs where flashbacks were quick narrations and APs where a flashback to one particular Descent was played over multiple sessions. DaveB had one going on that started with a flash-<i>forward</i>, the main campaign is a flashback, and there was a few sessions played in a flashback in the flashback. My own campaign started off in the late 1990s but is going to mostly take place in flashback to the 1920s. I've had other ideas of playing 2-3 sessions in each Sothic turn until we caught up to the present Sothic turn.[/QUOTE]<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seraph Kitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/296778-help-me-understand-timeline-assumptions-for-a-chronicle#post296778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That question segues nicely into my second big question, which is about how character progression and spending experience works. It seems clear to me that flashback scenes make great opportunities to earn exp and justify buying new traits. It works well for those dramatic moments where the player reveals they have a secret advantage that turns the scene, as we're so fond of seeing in novels and such. But traits bought in the present (or the latest period timeline)... are they available in flashback scenes?<br /> A lot of social merits (allies, contacts) don't logically transfer across time periods; sanctity of merits would suggest that they always can be used, they just apply to different people each time period, but that seems like an odd allowance, given that it entails cultivating relationships, and Arisen don't always have the chance to. I guess their cults do, but it seems like a bit of stretch to have in the charter to secure certain kinds of social connections, prepared enough in advance that they won't freak out when a Sekhem Arisen calls in a favor during an unexpected awakening. There is the &quot;use your best judgment of when the merit's available&quot; philosophy - and that's fine, but it seems like it could also lead to a lot of re-configuring of merits.<br /> From the basic chronology of characters, it makes most sense for Arisen to have a linear experience gains / character growth. Narratively, it makes sense to put more challenging scenes later in the chronicle, no matter when in the timeline it's flashing to. Locking characters out of skills seems unfair and like a lot of bookkeeping, even if its reasonable in a chronological sense, and letting purchases in the future apply to the past seems weird.</div> </div> </div> </div>If a Mummy PC I was GMing for bought an Utterance in the 'present' Descent, I may or may not decide they had access to it in any given flashback. If they earned xp in a flashback and bought an Affinity, then in the 'present' I might say they've 're-remembered' the Affinity in question and let them keep it. A Mummy's Memory fluctuates like crazy, and powers come and go depending on their habits and needs during Descents.<br /> <br /> <br /> So, if I were you and not as comfortable in Mummy's scope, then don't even worry about all this. Just play in the modern, current Sothic turn and leave it at that. Give your PCs little snippets of flashback narrative scenes when they earn Sebayt experience or buy a dot of Memory. If you're feeling a bit more comfortable then start to dive in to what their previous Descent before this one was like. If you're feeling relatively courageous then maybe pick one other time period that's super important to your campaign.<br /> <br /> So your Mummies are chilling in the late 2000s for a Sothic turn. With three PCs, maybe one woke up five years ago for something, another woke up 20 years ago, and another hasn't been awake for 50 years. Start thinking and imagining what the 2005 Descent was for the one Mummy, along with the 1990s Descent and the 1950s Descent. Then think back to another time they were all awake in the 1600s, and maybe spend an entire session or two playing through it. Maybe they're already along their Descent. If their current Mummies are still at Sekhem 6, maybe they're Sekhem 2 in the flashback. Maybe they have a power they don't have in the present day. Maybe give that power to them for free when the flashback is over!<br /> <br /> <b>Mummy</b> is a game served well by slow, personal reveals. You can be patient and your players won't even know - they'll just be excited, and shocked, to learn their characters' unique histories <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-30T06:57:35-07:00","id":297080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seraph Kitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/296778-help-me-understand-timeline-assumptions-for-a-chronicle#post296778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Greetings all,<br /> The character creation rules finish off the process by giving 20 exp to starting characters; like always, they recommend giving more to &quot;higher level&quot; games - 75 is for veterans of the Descent. Do these guidelines assume the main story is going to be in the 2012 Sothic Cycle? I would think that any Arisen that makes it to the 4th cycle would be considered a veteran, and have reached higher Memory... I would think this is just the typical guide for any game: &quot;whenever you're running, this is the default; this is for higher-powered&quot;. But part of me is thinking that it would make sense to give, like, 20 starting experience per Sothic Cycle they've been through, or something. Maybe I'm reading too much into things. But it gets to another question.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Veterans of Descents&quot; is a bit of a strange one. My personal interpretation has always been that they indicate Mummies that have learnt a lot from their Descents. It probably suggests an above-average Memory, or at least a large amount of retention despite forgetting where they learned those skills. It really isn't indicative of how active a Mummy has been.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seraph Kitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/296778-help-me-understand-timeline-assumptions-for-a-chronicle#post296778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>Mummy</i> uses flashbacks a lot as part of its storytelling style; I'm wondering how extensive that is. For example, would it make sense (or is it the default assumption) that the overarching story of a chronicle would take place in 2012, but mostly be used as framing; most of the individual stories taking place in past descents, skipping up and down the timeline to whatever short story arc is interesting, while seeding knowledge about the overarching conflict, resolved far in the present?<br /> Or is it intended that flashbacks are brief but frequent - primarily scenes of self-discovery, but often plot relevant - however the story arcs themselves and the bulk of plot / dramatic action occurs on the same timeline?<br /> While I'm certain that both of these styles of game are possible, I'm curious what the core <i>assumes</i> as the default, and which might take extra tinkering and present some clashes with the published material.</div> </div> </div> </div>The core suggests each as entirely valid ways of playing the game. I tend towards keeping the focus on the present with occasional playable flashbacks, but that's purely my preference. The core probably gives more support towards playing the game that way, but it's easily possible to have a much more flashback intensive game.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seraph Kitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/296778-help-me-understand-timeline-assumptions-for-a-chronicle#post296778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That question segues nicely into my second big question, which is about how character progression and spending experience works. It seems clear to me that flashback scenes make great opportunities to earn exp and justify buying new traits. It works well for those dramatic moments where the player reveals they have a secret advantage that turns the scene, as we're so fond of seeing in novels and such. But traits bought in the present (or the latest period timeline)... are they available in flashback scenes?<br /> A lot of social merits (allies, contacts) don't logically transfer across time periods; sanctity of merits would suggest that they always can be used, they just apply to different people each time period, but that seems like an odd allowance, given that it entails cultivating relationships, and Arisen don't always have the chance to. I guess their cults do, but it seems like a bit of stretch to have in the charter to secure certain kinds of social connections, prepared enough in advance that they won't freak out when a Sekhem Arisen calls in a favor during an unexpected awakening. There is the &quot;use your best judgment of when the merit's available&quot; philosophy - and that's fine, but it seems like it could also lead to a lot of re-configuring of merits.<br /> From the basic chronology of characters, it makes most sense for Arisen to have a linear experience gains / character growth. Narratively, it makes sense to put more challenging scenes later in the chronicle, no matter when in the timeline it's flashing to. Locking characters out of skills seems unfair and like a lot of bookkeeping, even if its reasonable in a chronological sense, and letting purchases in the future apply to the past seems weird.</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically, in flashbacks, players have a set of character traits that I have written up for them. They are entirely divorced from their traits in the present, although they are informed by them a little bit. The Memory system and the way Affinities work means that Skills, Affinities, and Utterances are all easily justified in the past no matter what the Mummy has in the present.<br /> <br /> If the flashbacks are the actual focus of the chronicle, I'd probably make sure that traits are a lot more closely linked in the past to what the players spend experience on. As reseru says, it's easily justified as a &quot;remembering that you once had this trait, so now you have always once had this trait&quot; kinda schtick. In a few of the flashbacks I run though, part of the fun is seeing the players react to how different they were mechanically in a past life.<br /> <br /> Social merits depend a bit on the Mummy. Because Mummy actually predates the Sanctity of Merits rule from GMC, there's an entire Affinity dedicated to making sure you can repurchase lost merits for free with minimal justification (Fate conspires to restore your Contacts, that kinda thing).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}